    70,
    $wb_book$[{"id":"welcome","title":"Welcome and Commitment","content":"Content Strategy 2025 Worksheet\nWelcome!\nSeriously, welcome to what might be the most important 30 days of your creative life.\nBecause this?\nThis isn’t another “content calendar.\n”\nThis isn’t some one-size-fits-all blueprint made for influencers with teams of editors.\nAnd this definitely isn’t about posting just to post.\nThis is about you showing the f*ck up, Loudly. Consistently. With purpose.\nFor yourself. For your audience. For what you’re building next.\nOver the next few pages, you're going to build a system that actually makes sense:\nNot just content ideas, but content identity .\nNot just trends, but traction .\nNot just goals, but momentum you can feel .\nBut listen, this is only gonna work if you decide, right now , that you’re all in.\n\nYou don’t need more motivation.\nYou don’t need another book to highlight and forget.\nYou need to do the thing.\nYou need to show up when it’s not cute.\nYou need to post when it’s not perfect.\nAnd you need to commit to being consistent, even if nobody claps yet.\nBecause that’s how the game changes.\nThat’s how brands are born.\nThat’s how you go from “posting online” to “building something real.\n”\nSo if you're here, really here, then lock in now:\nSay it out loud:\n\"I’m doing this. All of it. No half-assing. No disappearing. I’m building something real and I’m starting now.\n\"\nCool? Let’s build!"},{"id":"stage-1","title":"Stage 1: Identify The Plan","content":"Stage 1 - Identify The Plan\n🔥 Step 1: Pick ONE Primary Goal for the\nNext 30-180 Days\nYou’re not building an empire in a week. You’re building momentum.\nPick ONE of the following that matches where you're at now:\n🚀 GROW FOLLOWERS\nRecommended if you're starting from scratch or rebuilding.\nThis is the best place to start.\nYou’ll build your voice, your confidence, and your audience.\nLater, you can turn those followers into leads, customers, or fans.\n✅ Fastest to implement\n✅ Requires no tech setup\n✅ Builds proof + reach organically\n📥 COLLECT EMAILS\nRecommended if you have a freebie, group, or offer coming soon.\nThis is your backup plan if you’re kinda-sorta ready for business.\nJust make sure your CTA (link in bio, etc.) points somewhere useful.\n✅ Helps build future launch list\n✅ Pairs well with value content\n✅ Doesn’t require a full funnel (a Carrd + Mailchimp is enough)\n💰 SELL A PRODUCT OR SERVICE\nRecommended if your business is already running.\nIf you’ve got something to sell and people to help, cool.\nBut this path requires more setup and pressure.\n\n✅ Possible to start hot\n❌ Not ideal for beginners\n❌ Requires landing page, VSL, order form, follow-up, etc.\n⚠ Real Talk: If you don’t know what to sell yet, or you’re still refining your message, pick “Grow Followers.\n”\nYou’ll get clarity faster. You’ll build proof while you learn.\nEverything else becomes easier later.\n🧠 Step 2: Make a Mini Goal You Can See\nThis isn't about revenue projections or follower counts in a vacuum.\nPick something realistic, visible, and motivating .\nWrite your mini-goal for the next 30 days:\n\"By the end of 30 days, I want to...\n\"\n🟦 Post every day, even if it’s not perfect\n🟦 Gain 100 new followers across all platforms\n🟦 Start getting comments and replies on my videos\n🟦 Send 25 people to my freebie / group / offer\n🟦 Prove to myself I can actually do this\nYou don’t have to go viral. You just have to post .\n\n📌 Step 3: Commit to the Routine, Not the\nResults\nThis month is about building the muscle , not hitting the numbers.\nAnswer this out loud or write it down:\n\"If I only had one job for the next 30 days, it would be to...\n\" (Example: “Post 1 video per day and reply to 5 comments.\n”)\nNow finish this:\n\"And if I do that, I trust that...\n\" (Example: “\n...my content will get better and people will start noticing.\n”)\n✅ Wrap-Up\nYou just set a clear goal and a commitment you can keep.\n●\n●\n●\nDon’t worry about revenue yet\nDon’t worry about your logo\nDon’t stress over perfect hooks or funnels\nYou’re building a daily habit. That’s the win."},{"id":"stage-2","title":"Stage 2: Set the Stage","content":"🛠 STAGE 2: SET THE STAGE (ONLINE SETUP)\nLook like you mean it, because strangers will check.\n🧠 Step 1: Match Your Setup to Your Goal (Refer back to Stage 1, this changes what you focus on now.)\nIf your goal is to GROW FOLLOWERS:\n✅ Make your profile pic clean, bold, and recognizable\n✅ Use your name or username consistently across platforms\n✅ Write a 1-line description that says:\nWho you are → What they’ll get → Why to follow\nExample: “Burned-out worker turned full-time creator. Helping you escape the grind.\n”\n✅ Add a simple CTA:\n“Follow for content that [does what]”\n✅ Pin a featured post or video that introduces you (or your vibe)\nIf your goal is to COLLECT EMAILS:\n✅ Do all of the above\n✅ Set up a landing page with your opt-in (Carrd is perfect for this)\n✅ Connect your email tool (Mailchimp, ConvertKit, etc.)\n✅ Write a simple welcome email that delivers your freebie or says hi\n\n✅ Test the form yourself, make sure it works\nIf your goal is to SELL SOMETHING:\n✅ Do all of the above\n✅ Create a simple sales page (even if it’s just a link to book a call or buy your product)\n✅ Make sure your checkout process works\n✅ If you're using a funnel → test every step like a buyer\n✅ Optional: Add testimonials or product highlights to your featured content\n📋 Step 2: Run the Profile Clean-Up Checklist\nNo matter what your goal is, these still matter:\n✅ Profile pic is recent, high contrast, not chaotic\n✅ Name + handle match across platforms\n✅ Bio/description has ONE clear CTA (freebie, group, etc.)\n✅ Link in bio is live and points to your main action\n✅ Featured post/pinned video is intentional and fresh\n✅ Bonus: Update your banner/header with your main theme or brand phrase\n✅ Bonus: Add a welcome post or video in your Facebook group / YouTube\n“You don’t need to look like a brand. But you need to look like a real person who didn’t just make this account last night.\n”*\n✍ Step 3: Write Your Bio + CTA\nLet’s knock this out fast. Use this fill-in-the-blank to draft your short bio:\n\nI help [who] do/escape/understand [what], so they can [outcome].\nFollow me for [topic or vibe], new stuff daily.\nExample:\nI help burned-out employees escape retail and launch online.\nFollow for tips, rants, and no-BS business strategy.\nNow your CTA (what you want them to click):\n“Get the free checklist”\n“Join my free challenge”\n“Watch the video that started it all”\n“See how I make my content in under 1 hour/day”\n🎯 Your Goal This Week:\n✅ Clean up your profiles\n✅ Choose where your link sends people\n✅ Post or pin something that gives them a reason to stay\n✅ Bonus: Ask a friend to click your link and DM you what they see"},{"id":"stage-3","title":"Stage 3: Pick Your Content Types","content":"🎥 STAGE 3: PICK YOUR\nCONTENT TYPES\nPost what fits your energy, your brand, and your current level of sanity.\n🎯 Step 1: Understand Why Variety Matters\nYou don’t need to do everything, but if every video feels the same, people scroll past.\n●\n●\n●\nVariety keeps your audience curious\nIt helps you stay interested\nDifferent platforms reward different formats\nYour goal here isn’t to go viral. It’s to get noticed enough to matter .\n\n📋 Step 2: The Content Menu (Pick What Fits)\nGo through the list and mark the ones that feel like you . Not what sounds cool. Not what “performs.\n” What you’ll actually do without quitting next week.\n✅ 🎙 Talking Head-Teach, rant, explain, story-tell\n✅ 🎭 Skits / Characters / Chaos Shorts-High-energy storytelling or absurd takes\n✅ 💡 Tips & Tutorials-Quick wins they can try today\n✅ 🎬 Trend-Based-Use viral audios or formats\n✅ 📹 Duets & Replies-React to comments, stitch takes\n✅ 👣 Day-in-the-Life / BTS-Show your work, your vibe\n✅ 📋 Daily To-Do / “Skip if You’re Not Me”-Public accountability\n✅ 🧠 Hot Takes & Bold Opinions-Stir up engagement\n✅ 🌀 Themed Series-30-day challenges, daily truths, etc.\n✅ 📚 Proof / Screenshots / Testimonials-Show results without bragging\n✅ 🤖 Faceless AI Videos\n✅ 😅 Reaction Content-Stories, facts, Reddit/Quora voiceovers-Reddit threads, niche memes, bad advice breakdowns\n🔁 Bonus: Repurpose Like a Pro\nEven if you don’t have time to make something new:\n●\nCut your old YouTube, podcast, or livestreams into short clips\n●\nRepost a banger from 6 months ago your new followers missed\n●\nRecord the same topic 3 different ways (serious, silly, soft) to see what hits\n●\nAdd new captions, crop it differently, change the hook, easy refresh\n\n🧠 Step 3: Choose Your Core 2 (Start Simple)\nPick two formats you’ll focus on this month. These are your “go-to” styles when you’re tired, busy, or uninspired.\nWrite them here:\n1.\n2.\nNow pick one format you’ll experiment with just for fun:\n🎲 Wildcard Format:\n___________________________\nYou can change these later, but only after you actually try them.\nDo each format at least 3-5 times before deciding it's “not for you.\n”\n✅ Step 4: Commit to Consistency\nChoose your output level based on your schedule and energy.\n●\n●\n●\n●\n🟢 Trial Mode -\n🟢 Basic Mode\n🔵\nGrowth Mode\n🔴 Beast Mode\n1 post/week-3 posts/week-5 posts/week-Daily (or 2x/day)\nWrite your commitment:\n“I’m going to post\n___ videos per week for the next 30 days.\n”\nNow screenshot this. Set a reminder. Tell a friend.\nThis is where it starts to get real."},{"id":"stage-4","title":"Stage 4: Build a Weekly Flow","content":"📆 STAGE 4: BUILD A WEEKLY\nFLOW (THAT FITS YOUR TIME)\nYou don’t need a content calendar. You need a rhythm that works.\n⚠ Real Talk: Expect it to take longer than it “should”\nIf you think filming a 60-second video will take 5 minutes, you’re gonna get big mad and quit.\nHere’s the truth:\n●\n●\n●\n●\n●\nPosting daily will stretch you at first\nIt will feel messy\nYou’ll want to restart every video 10 times\nEditing will make you want to throw your phone\nIt will take HOURS for a single short video at first\nThat’s normal.\nIt gets faster. You get sharper.\nBut you only improve by posting bad stuff consistently .\nNot by waiting until it’s “ready.\n”\n🧠 Step 1: Your Realistic Daily Workflow\nStart with a basic 2-3 hour block per day (can be split up).\n📹 45 mins-Film 1-2 videos\n\n●\n●\nOne can be quick, raw, shoot-and-post\nOne should have more intention (scripted, or thought-through)\n✂ 30 mins- 2 hours-Edit, caption, and upload\n●\n●\nUse built-in platform tools or CapCut\nPost to 3-4 platforms (batch your captions if needed)\n💬 60 mins-Reply to comments + DMs\n●\n●\n●\nPrioritize real convos\nUse voice replies if you're quick\nIf someone asks something good, turn it into your next post\n👀 60 mins-Scroll with purpose\n●\n●\n●\nLeave meaningful comments on other creators\nStitch or duet viral takes with your own spin\nDebate something real in your niche\nYou don’t have to do every part of this every day.\nBut if you’re short on time, do this:\nPost something → Reply to every comment → Leave 3 comments on others' content\nDone.\n\n📅 Step 2: Set Your Weekly Theme Flow\nGive your brain a break. Theme your days.\nHere’s a sample flow:\n🟩 Monday-PLAN\n●\n●\n●\nPick 3-5 topics\nSave 2-3 trending audios\nChoose your “proof” moment for the week\n🟦 Tuesday-SCRIPT\n●\nWrite or outline 1-2 Talking Head videos\n●\nBullet a skit or trend idea if you're doing one\n●\nDraft captions if you like batching\n🟨 Wednesday-FILM + POST\n●\nRecord everything in one block if possible\n●\nPost 1 fun piece + 1 authority piece\n●\nSave drafts if needed\n🟥 Thursday-COMMENT WAR\n●\nGo debate someone respectfully (or not)\n●\nStitch or duet viral takes\n●\nDrop opinions in other people’s comment sections\n🟧 Friday-STORYTIME or PROOF\n●\nTalk about a result, lesson, or transformation\n●\nShare something real (screenshot, DM, moment)\n🟪 Saturday-CHILL CONTENT + COMMUNITY\n●\n●\nReply to comments\nShare a “lazy” day post\n\n●\nAsk your audience a question or let them vote\n⬜ Sunday-RECAP + BTS\n●\nReflect on the week\n●\nShow behind-the-scenes or your setup\n●\nFilm your “Skip if You’re Not Me” or to-do list\n✅ Step 3: Write Your Custom Flow\nUse the sample above or write your own.\nMy Weekly Content Rhythm:\nMon:\nTue:\nWed:\nThu:\nFri:\nSat:\nSun:\n______________________________\n______________________________\n______________________________\n______________________________\n______________________________\n______________________________\n______________________________\nNow write this somewhere bold:\n“Done is better than perfect. Posted is better than planned.\n”"},{"id":"stage-5","title":"Stage 5: Engage to Grow","content":"💬 STAGE 5: ENGAGE TO GROW\nPosting is step one. Growth happens when you stop yelling and start listening.\n🧠 Step 1: Forget the Metrics That Don’t\nMatter\nNo one cares how many likes you got if no one knows your name.\nYou do not need to track follower count, view averages, or conversion rates.\nHere’s your new metric:\nDid people respond to your stuff?\n●\n●\nIf yes → reply\nIf no → go start a conversation\nThis isn’t about managing a brand. It’s about showing up in the room where your people already hang out.\n\n🔁 Step 2: Weekly Engagement Habits (Your\nReal Growth Plan)\nYou don’t need a spreadsheet.\nYou need a routine of interaction . Do these every week:\n✅ Reply to every single comment\n●\n●\n●\nYes, even the dumb ones\nUse voice replies if you want speed + human connection\nDon’t let haters get the last word, confront BS with confidence\n✅ Follow every creator with similar content\n●\n●\n●\nTheir audience is your audience\nLurk their comments and stitch the best takes\nUse their ideas as launch pads, not competition\n✅ Comment on 3-5 videos every day\n●\n●\n●\nAdmire them? Say why.\nDisagree? Say it better.\nStart debates, ask better questions, or drop one-liners\n✅ Duet or Stitch 1 hot take per week\n●\n●\n●\nAdd your spin, your version, or your correction\nDo it fast, don’t wait 3 days “scripting”\nHot takes get shared. Jump on them.\n\n✅ Join conversations in groups or threads\nFind niche Facebook groups, YouTube comment wars, or Reddit\n● threads\n●\n●\nDrop answers better than the original creator\nGive away knowledge like candy\n✅ Share content from another creator\n●\n●\n●\nScreenshot, duet, or tag them\nIt shows you're plugged in, not isolated\nBonus: it starts relationships\n✅ DM 1 person per week you admire\n●\n●\n●\nKeep it real, not awkward:\n“Been loving your stuff lately, how’s it going over there?”\nMight turn into a collab, a shoutout, or a future feature\nMight also lead nowhere, and that’s fine too\n🎯 Step 3: Make Engagement a\nNon-Negotiable\nWrite your own Engagement Checklist for the week:\n●\n●\n●\n●\n●\nReplied to all comments\nLeft 5+ new comments\nFollowed 2 new niche creators\nStitched or dueted 1 video\nShared someone else’s content\n\n●\nDMed 1 person\nEven if you don’t post, do these.\nIf you’re burned out on filming, engagement keeps you visible.\n✅ Wrap-Up Reminder\nYou’re not here to be an algorithm slave.\nYou’re here to build trust, stir thought, and grow a name that matters.\nYou’re not “posting content.\n”\nYou’re building a voice inside an ecosystem."},{"id":"conclusion","title":"Conclusion and Next Steps","content":"Conclusion: Congrats! You just did what 99% of people never will.\nIf you’ve made it this far, take a breath and really let this sink in:\nYou showed up. You didn’t skim. You didn’t scroll past. You actually sat down and built a full 5-stage content strategy, one that’s simple, honest, and built for real people with real lives, not influencers with teams and trust funds.\nThat’s huge.\nLet’s not gloss over it. You now have:\n●\nA clear outcome that actually matters to you\n●\nAn online setup that won’t embarrass you when people click\n●\nA menu of content formats that fit your personality, not some algorithm god\n●\nA weekly flow that respects your time and still gets results\n●\nAnd a game plan for engagement that goes beyond “just post and pray”\nYou built it. You did the work. You have the tools.\nAnd now comes the most dangerous part of any journey:\nTrying to do it all alone.\nLook, I know how this story goes, because I’ve lived it. You get hyped. You map it all out. You do a few posts. Maybe a few more. And then life hits.\nYou get behind. You miss a day. Then another. You stop replying. You stop showing up. And the dream you swore you were going to make happen? It fades into another folder of “someday.\n”\n\nI don’t want that for you. Not after how far you’ve come.\nSo here’s what I’m offering:\nIf you’re serious about showing up daily, building content with a purpose, and finally making progress on your business or creative brand, I want to be in your corner.\n✅ Daily support\n✅ Strategic feedback\n✅ Actual partnership, not “coaching” from the sidelines\n✅ A custom plan that grows with you\n✅ And a real human who gives a sh*t about your momentum\nThis is my private program for creators, coaches, and solopreneurs who want to Build With Bee . It’s not a course. It’s not a passive membership.\nIt’s a collaboration .\nYou bring the goals.\nI bring the experience.\nTogether, we build the system you’ll actually stick with.\nGo now to ColoradoMastermind.com/#BuildWithBee\n●\n●\n●\n●\nClick the link. Fill out the quick form.\nAnd let’s see if we’re a fit.\nIf we are, I’ll personally help you:\nRefine your offers\nFix your funnel flow\nMap your content around your actual schedule\nAnd finally get you making consistent money from the content you’re already trying to create\nBecause you weren’t meant to build this alone.\n\nYou made it to the end of this guide. That says something about you.\nIt means you’re done sitting on the sidelines.\nIt means you’re hungry.\nIt means you’re ready.\nSo don’t ghost your momentum.\nDon’t let this turn into another “almost.\n”\nLet’s build your audience.\nLet’s build your business.\nLet’s build your next 30 days, together.\n👉\nColoradoMastermind.com/#BuildWithBee\nI’ll see you on the other side."}]$wb_book$::jsonb
  ),
  (
    $wb_book$social-media-reply-strategy$wb_book$,
    $wb_book$David Bee's Social Media Reply Strategy$wb_book$,
    $wb_book$Five Steps to Stand Out and Start Real Conversations$wb_book$,
    $wb_book$How to answer public comments with empathy, insight, and thoughtful questions that help people feel seen and open genuine DMs without pitching.$wb_book$,
    'web',
    'published',
    '/assets/eee/vault.png',
    90,
    $wb_book$[{"id":"purpose","title":"Why This Reply Strategy Works","content":"David Bee’s Social Media Reply Strategy:\n The goal of this is to stand out from anyone who has ever commented on their posts before.\n You’re not just dropping a generic “sending hugs” or a quick tip, they’ve probably seen a\n hundred of those. You have to give better advice , be more empathetic , and provide real,\n tangible value immediately .\n This isn’t a sneaky way to get a DM. You’re not selling anything. You’re literally just showing up\n for them in a way they never expected from a stranger on the internet . Your response\n should be so thoughtful, so deeply understanding, that they feel a real moment of relief just\n from reading it .\n But it’s not just about helping them . These are public posts, potentially seen by thousands of\n people . When you craft an incredible response, you’re not just making a difference for one\n person; you’re making an impression on everyone reading . Other people will start to notice,\n respect, and remember you as\n\"the person who actually helps people.\n\"\n And here’s the secret: People love following along with a story . When someone shares their\n pain, others watch to see how the conversation unfolds. When your response is genuinely\n insightful and caring , you’re not just answering one person, you’re creating a moment that\n draws others in . They’ll remember you. They’ll trust you. And when they need help, guess who\n they’ll think of first?\n That’s the power of showing up with real, human support.\n💛\n\n When responding to a Facebook comment with the goal of getting them to DM you back, the\n process is to acknowledge the person’s struggles, offer insight, and subtly encourage a deeper\n conversation, without directly asking them to message you (since group rules may not allow it).\n When someone is going through emotional distress they don’t just need advice, they need to\n feel seen, understood, and supported . Your response should be warm, personal, and leave\n the door open for a deeper conversation.\n Follow these steps to create a thoughtful and engaging response:\n Step 1: Acknowledge Their Pain and Bravery\n This person is likely struggling, so the first step is to honor how hard this is for them ."},{"id":"five-steps","title":"The Five-Step Reply Framework","content":"🔹 Example:\n If they say:\n“I’m really struggling emotionally after my surgery. I feel exhausted all the time, and\n it’s hard not to feel hopeless.\n”\n You might start with:\n\n\"That sounds so incredibly tough. Surgery takes such a huge toll, not just on the body, but\n emotionally too. And when you're feeling exhausted all the time, it’s so easy to start feeling\n hopeless. I really feel for you.\n\"\n Step 2: Restate Their Struggle in a Way That Shows Deep Understanding\n This helps them feel like you get what they’re going through, maybe even better than they can\n explain it.\n 🔹 Example:\n\n\"It’s not just about the physical pain, right? It’s that feeling of being stuck, like your body won’t\n cooperate, and you’re not sure when, or if, you’ll feel like yourself again.\n\"\n\n Step 3: Offer a Gentle, Helpful Insight\n Since they’re already overwhelmed, keep this part light and supportive, no pressure, just\n something comforting.\n 🔹 Example:\n\n\"Something that can help is reminding yourself that healing isn’t a straight line. Some days will\n feel better, some won’t, but no single day defines your whole recovery. Your body is working on\n healing, even if it doesn’t feel like it.\n\"\n\nStep 4: Ask a Thought-Provoking Question to Keep the Conversation Going\n This invites them to reflect and respond, which builds connection.\n 🔹 Example:\n\n\"When do you feel even a little relief, mentally or physically? Even small moments count.\n\"\n\n Step 5: Subtly Suggest That Support Can Make a Huge Difference\n Without saying\n“message me,\n”\n hint that they don’t have to go through this alone.\n 🔹 Example:\n\n\"I know that when people are in the middle of something this heavy, it can feel like no one truly\n understands. But I promise, you’re not alone, and you don’t have to figure this out by yourself.\n\"\n Step 6: End with an Open-Ended Thought That Invites a Deeper\n Conversation\n Encourage them to take the next step in talking more if they need it.\n 🔹 Example:\n\n\"If you ever want to talk through what’s been the hardest part for you, I’m here. Sometimes, just\n having someone to check in with makes all the difference.\n\"\n\n Final Example: A Full /DM Response in Action\n Comment:\n\n\"I’m really struggling emotionally after my surgery. I feel exhausted all the time, and it’s hard not\n to feel hopeless.\n\"\n Reply:\n\n\"That sounds so incredibly tough. Surgery takes such a huge toll, not just on the body, but\n emotionally too. And when you're feeling exhausted all the time, it’s so easy to start feeling\n hopeless. I really feel for you.\n\n\"It’s not just about the physical pain, right? It’s that feeling of being stuck, like your body won’t\n cooperate, and you’re not sure when, or if, you’ll feel like yourself again.\n\n\"Something that can help is reminding yourself that healing isn’t a straight line. Some days will\n feel better, some won’t, but no single day defines your whole recovery. Your body is working on\n healing, even if it doesn’t feel like it.\n\n\"When do you feel even a little relief, mentally or physically? Even small moments count."},{"id":"examples","title":"Real Comment and Reply Examples","content":"\"I know that when people are in the middle of something this heavy, it can feel like no one truly\n understands. But I promise, you’re not alone, and you don’t have to figure this out by yourself.\n If you ever want to talk through what’s been the hardest part for you, I’m here. Sometimes, just\n having someone to check in with makes all the difference.\n\"\n\n Actual Examples David has implemented:\n Comment : This event was so intense, I found myself so overwhelmed at times I started\n crying. Honestly, I think I’m not able to do this. It’s too hard and feels impossible. Does\n anyone else feel this way?\n David’s Reply: Oh wow, first of all, I just want to acknowledge how brave you are for sharing\n this. That summit was intense, and if you found yourself weeping through it, that just means it hit\n something deep for you. You're not the only one who struggled with the exercises, I’ve heard\n from so many people who felt overwhelmed, stuck, or even a little lost afterward.\n It’s not that you’re “not able for this.\n” If anything, your emotional reaction shows that you are\n capable, you’re just hitting a block that needs a different approach. Sometimes, the problem\n isn’t the exercises themselves but trying to do them alone without the right kind of support.\n A really interesting question to ask yourself is: What exactly felt impossible? Was it the emotions\n that came up, the structure of the exercises, or feeling unsure if you were “doing it right”?\n Because once we figure that out, we can make it work for you instead of against you.\n A lot of people think they just need more strategies, but in reality, the missing piece is often\n accountability and support, someone to help process those big emotions and guide them\n through the steps in a way that actually clicks. If that sounds like something you’d want to\n explore, I’d love to chat more about it. You're definitely not alone in this.\n Comment : Hi. I bought last years UPW but ended up not playing full out. Did it virtually.\n Wasted money. I’ve spent thousands of dollars on MasterMind with different academy\n programs and haven’t accomplished a thing.\n I read so many people’s testimonies about how they succeeded. I don’t have an excuse. I\n don’t have a really sad story.\n Don’t get me wrong, I’m grateful.\n I do need to attend, there are dreams I would like to accomplish: podcast, marathon\n training program, running app in Spanish, make money to retire my siblings and take\n care of my parents.\n I also have a friend who’s a single mom who can really use this but she’s not motivated. I\n have this idea of inviting her and going together and playing full out but I’m afraid it will\n just be more waste of money. 😒\n\nHow do I make progress guys?! I don’t have an excuse!!! There are so many of you who\n have gone through so much!!! ❤\n David’s Reply : Hey, first I just want to acknowledge how honest and self-aware you are. That\n alone puts you ahead of most people. You don’t have a tragic backstory, but that doesn’t mean\n your dreams and challenges aren’t real. And let’s be honest, spending money on programs and\n still feeling stuck? That’s frustrating. You’re not alone in that.\n Here’s what stands out to me: You *do* have a vision. A podcast, a marathon program, a\n running app in Spanish, taking care of your family, that’s not the mindset of someone who’s\n lost. That’s the mindset of someone who *wants* more but maybe hasn’t found the right way to\n execute yet. The desire is there.\n So, here’s a question: Instead of looking at past investments as wasted money, what if you\n looked at them as training? What did you learn, about yourself, about what *doesn’t* work for\n you? Because knowing that is powerful.\n And about bringing your friend, sometimes, when we focus on pulling someone else up, it\n prevents *us* from moving forward. What if you committed to playing full out for *you* first? You\n can inspire her by taking action instead of waiting for her to be ready.\n Maybe what you need isn’t another program, it’s accountability, momentum, and a plan that\n works for *you*\n. Have you ever had someone walk with you step by step to break through that\n feeling of being stuck? Because sometimes, that’s the missing piece. I know a lot of people\n have replied to you already with great advice, but I'm happy to chat if you're up for it.\n Comment : Ok mastermind folks! Need some advice, I FINALLY recorded my first real\n authentic video on how I want to help my niche community, but it is 15 mins long and I'm\n not sure where to post it. I don't have a Youtube channel. I do have an Instagram and\n Facebook page. I don't know if posting a 15 min video on Instagram is even feasible or\n recommended? Please would love some advice. I thought about editing it, but honestly I\n want to stay as authentic as possible and I feel like I should just post as is, considering\n it's my first ever video. If I start trying to perfect it, then I won't post it at all. Thanks!\n David’s Reply: WOW!!!!! Huge congrats on recording your first real, authentic video! That’s a\n huge step, and I love that you’re focused on staying true to your message.\n Since IGTV was phased out a few years ago, Instagram now keeps all videos under the main\n\n\"Instagram Video\" format. You \"can\" upload a 15-minute video there, but Instagram tends to\n favor shorter content for engagement. Facebook, on the other hand, is a great spot for longer\n videos, especially if your audience is already there, and then you don't have to worry about\n trying to set up a Youtube Channel\n\nHere’s something to think about: What’s the main goal of this video? If it’s to introduce yourself\n and connect with your niche, Facebook might be the best place to post since it supports longer\n content and deeper conversation. (like we're having right now!)\n There are a lot of technical strategies out there, but sometimes what makes the biggest\n difference is having support in making confident, strategic decisions. If you ever want to talk\n through it, I’d be happy to help!"}]$wb_book$::jsonb
  ),
  (
    $wb_book$filming-video-shorts-guide$wb_book$,
    $wb_book$In-Depth Guide to Filming Effective Video Shorts with Hooks$wb_book$,
    $wb_book$Hooks, Client Avatars, and Short-Form Filming Checklist$wb_book$,
    $wb_book$A practical guide to opening short videos in the first three seconds, transitioning into natural conversation, speaking to specific client avatars, and shipping three times a week.$wb_book$,
    'web',
    'published',
    '/assets/eee/vault.png',
    100,
    $wb_book$[{"id":"why-hooks","title":"Why the Hook Matters","content":"In-Depth Guide to Filming Effective Video Shorts with Hooks\nWhy the Hook Matters:\nWhen creating video content, especially for platforms like TikTok or Instagram Reels, the first few seconds are crucial. This is the time when viewers decide whether to keep watching or swipe away. If you don't grab their attention immediately, the platform's algorithm will quickly deem your video unengaging and reduce its reach, meaning fewer people will see it.\nIt might feel uncomfortable or unnatural to structure your videos with a predefined hook, especially if you're used to just vibing and letting conversations flow. But there’s a good reason for this approach. By ensuring your video starts strong, you increase the chances of holding your audience’s attention and getting more views. This is key to building momentum and spreading your message effectively.\nHow to Get Comfortable with This Approach:\nThe good news is that you don’t have to abandon your natural style entirely. Think of the hook as a framework, a tool to set the stage. Once you’ve captured attention, you can relax into the conversation, just like you would normally.\nHere’s how to ease into it:\n1. Start with a Strong Hook: Begin your video with a clear and engaging statement that addresses a specific concern or emotion your audience might be feeling. This is the part where you might feel a bit scripted at first, but it’s important to get this right.\n2. Transition to Free-Flowing Conversation: After the hook, feel free to transition into a more natural style. Talk about what’s on your mind, share your thoughts, or engage with what’s happening around you. This is where your authentic voice can shine. But the key is to keep it under 90 seconds\n3. Practice Makes Perfect: It will take some time to get used to this format, but with practice, it will start to feel more natural. The goal is to integrate the hook into your flow so smoothly that it becomes second nature.\nWhy We’re Focusing on Hooks Right Now:\nThe hook is the most critical part of your video. Once you’ve mastered this, the rest will follow.\nThe reason we’re putting so much emphasis on the hook is because it’s the key to making sure your message reaches as many people as possible. The more comfortable you get with starting your videos this way, the more effective your content will be."},{"id":"framework","title":"Assignment and Hook Framework","content":"Your Assignment:\n●\n●\n●\n●\n●\nActivity-Based Filming: Start by filming yourself doing things you’re already comfortable with, like taking a walk, grabbing coffee, or interacting with people in your community. These activities provide a natural backdrop for your message and help you feel at ease on camera.\nFocus on the Hook: Each time you start a video, use the framework outlined below to craft your hook. Don’t worry if it feels a bit rehearsed at first; it will get easier with practice.\nLet the Rest Flow Naturally: After you’ve delivered your hook, transition into whatever you want to talk about. Share your thoughts, reflect on current events, or discuss how the activity you’re doing ties back to the message you’re sharing.\nHashtags and Posting Tips:\n○\nUse up to 6 relevant hashtags per post (e.g., #Stress, #Trauma, #Money).\n○\nAim to post at least 2-3 times per week, (once or twice a day is ideal for the algorithm, but it’s more important to have a consistent schedule than to post daily)\nChallenge Yourself: Film these videos while you’re on the go, walking, grabbing a coffee, reading headlines, or interacting with others. Include a custom call to action (CTA) related to the activity, tying it back to your primary message.\nHook Framework for Entrepreneurs\n1. Identify the Pain Point : Address a specific frustration or need your audience may experience.\nExample: “Tired of working hard without seeing real growth?”\n2. Highlight the Consequences of Inaction : Explain why staying on the same path won’t bring improvement.\nExample: “Relying on the same strategies will only keep you stuck.\n”\n3. Ask a Rhetorical Question or Open a Loop : Get the audience to reflect on the need for change.\nExample: “Isn’t it time to explore a new way forward?”\n4. Offer Yourself or Your Product as a Solution : Present your solution confidently and inspire action.\nExample: “I can help you break through these barriers, let’s start today.\n”\nExample Hook in Action\nPain Point : “Putting in the hours but still feeling stuck?”\nConsequence : “Without a strategy shift, nothing will change.\n”\nRhetorical Question : “Ready for a real breakthrough?”\nSolution : “Let’s create a roadmap to help you succeed.\n”"},{"id":"avatars-1","title":"Client Avatar Scripts: Entrepreneur & Divorce","content":"Creating Client Avatars\nCreating client avatars is essential for producing highly targeted, impactful content that resonates deeply with your audience. By understanding your clients’ unique struggles, personalities, and aspirations, you can craft messages that feel personalized and empathetic, which builds trust and increases engagement. With specific avatars in mind, you’ll be able to speak directly to their needs, making your content both more relevant and more effective. Here’s how to use this approach for three key avatars: Kathy the Overwhelmed Entrepreneur, Jenny the Recently Divorced, and Michelle the Socially Disconnected Introvert.\nScript 1\nHook:\n\"Do you ever feel like your business is running you instead of the other way around?\"\nPain Point:\n\"When you’re buried under all the roles and responsibilities of running a business solo, burnout can feel inevitable.\n\"\nEmpathy/Validation:\n\"I know how hard it is when every task feels essential, but it’s also impossible to do it all without losing your energy.\n\"\nSolution:\n\"Here’s a secret: setting boundaries can actually help your business thrive while protecting your mental health.\n\"\nCTA:\n\"Let’s talk about a few ways you can create those boundaries so your business works for you, not against you.\n\"\nScript 2\nHook:\nPain Point:\nEmpathy/Validation:\n\"If you’re wearing a dozen different hats in your business, you’re not alone.\n\"\n\"Running everything yourself can lead to burnout fast.\n\"\n\"Doing it all is a challenge many entrepreneurs face, and it can be draining.\n\"\nSolution:\n\"The good news is that taking even small steps to manage your workload can help you regain energy and refocus on what you love.\n\"\nCTA:\n\"In this video, I’ll share some practical tips to help you balance the load without compromising your vision or well-being.\n\"\nScript 3\nHook:\nPain Point: started it all.\n\"\nEmpathy/Validation:\nSolution: truly recharges you.\n\"\nCTA: alive.\n\"\n\"Is stress starting to steal the joy of being an entrepreneur?\"\n\"When every day feels like an uphill climb, it’s easy to lose sight of the passion that\n\"It’s natural to feel worn out when your to-do list never ends.\n\"\n\"Sometimes, the best way to grow your business is to step back and focus on what\n\"Let’s explore a few ways you can reignite your energy and keep that entrepreneurial spirit\nFor Jenny the Recently Divorced\n\nScript 1\nHook:\n\"Starting over after divorce isn’t easy, and if you’re feeling a bit lost, that’s completely normal.\n\"\nPain Point:\nEmpathy/Validation:\nSolution:\n\"After a major life change, it can be hard to feel steady and secure again.\n\"\n\"Divorce can feel like it takes away the foundation of who we are.\n\"\n\"But this can also be a time to rebuild yourself and focus on what truly brings you happiness.\n\"\nCTA:\n\"In this video, I’ll walk through some powerful ways you can find joy and confidence in this new chapter, one small step at a time.\n\"\nScript 2\nHook:\nPain Point:\n\"\n\"Being single again can feel overwhelming, especially if it’s been a while.\n\"\n\"Rediscovering yourself after a long relationship can feel confusing and even scary.\nEmpathy/Validation:\n\"It’s perfectly normal to feel this way. This is a huge transition, and you don’t have to have all the answers right away.\n\"\nSolution:\n\"Rediscovering yourself is a journey worth taking, and with the right steps, it can be fulfilling and transformative.\n\"\nCTA:\n\"Let’s dive into some ways to embrace this new independence and turn it into a time of growth and self-discovery.\n\"\nScript 3\nHook:\n\"Uncertainty about the future after divorce is completely normal, and you’re not alone in feeling that way.\n\"\nPain Point:\n\"It’s hard to know what’s next when the life you had changes so drastically.\n\"\nEmpathy/Validation:\n\"This time can feel unsettling, but you’re not alone, and you’re capable of creating a future you love.\n\"\nSolution:\n\"Think of this as a chance to redefine who you are and what makes you feel fulfilled.\n\"\nCTA:\n\"Today, I’ll share strategies to help you feel grounded and start shaping a life that’s meaningful and empowering.\n\"\nFor Michelle the Socially Disconnected Introvert\nScript 1\nHook:\n\"Feeling disconnected from others can be tough, especially when you’re naturally introverted.\n\"\nPain Point:\n\"It can feel almost impossible to connect with others when it seems like the world values extroversion.\n\"\nEmpathy/Validation:\n\"You don’t have to change who you are to find meaningful connections.\n\"\nSolution:\n\"Let’s talk about ways you can build relationships that feel supportive and genuine, without stepping too far out of your comfort zone.\n\"\nCTA:\n\"If you’re ready, let’s explore some ideas that can help you connect authentically with people who appreciate you for who you are.\n\""},{"id":"avatars-2","title":"Client Avatar Scripts: Introvert Connection","content":"Script 2\nHook: can feel tricky.\n\"\nPain Point:\nEmpathy/Validation: social energy is limited.\n\"\nSolution: someone you’re not.\n\"\nCTA: to who you are.\n\"\n\"If you’re an introvert, balancing your love of solitude with the need for social connection\n\"It’s hard to find people who truly understand and respect your need for space.\n\"\n\"Many introverts feel the same, it’s not easy to build connections when\n\"But there are ways to meet people who get you and won’t pressure you to be\n\"In this video, I’ll share ways to create relationships that are comfortable, natural, and true\nScript 3\nHook:\n\"Ever feel like you want companionship, but without the pressure to constantly be social?\"\nPain Point:\n\"Wanting connection without the overwhelming demand to be ‘on’ all the time is completely valid.\n\"\nEmpathy/Validation:\n\"Building connections that respect your need for space is key to feeling fulfilled without feeling drained.\n\"\nSolution:\n\"Let’s explore a few ways to balance solitude with meaningful social connections, so you feel supported but never overwhelmed.\n\"\nCTA:\n\"Stick with me, and I’ll show you how to maintain a healthy balance between alone time and companionship.\n\""},{"id":"checklist","title":"Posting Guidelines and Checklist","content":"Posting Guidelines\n●\n●\n●\n●\n●\n●\n●\n●\n●\nClear Audio : Ensure your voice is audible and background noise is minimal.\nGood Lighting : Shoot in a well-lit area to make sure you’re visible.\nAvoid the “Pause”\n: Start speaking as soon as the video begins; edit out any initial silence.\nImmediate Movement : Begin with motion to catch the viewer’s eye, walking, gesturing, or shaking the camera slightly.\nKeep It Short : Aim for 45-90 seconds to retain viewer interest.\nNatural Environment : Film in familiar settings, like while walking, having coffee, or engaging with people around you.\nEngaging Thumbnails : Use an attention-grabbing thumbnail.\nConsistent Posting : Post at least 3 times a week to build momentum.\nCustom CTA : Connect your call-to-action (CTA) to the video message.\nConclusion: Mastering Video Shorts with Hooks\nA structured hook helps create compelling content without losing your unique voice. By drawing viewers in with a strong opening, you can let your true personality and message resonate. This approach keeps your content authentic and impactful.\nNext Steps :\n1. Practice Your Hook Framework : Integrate the hook naturally into your videos.\n2. Post Regularly : Commit to at least three posts per week for consistent engagement.\n3. Refine and Improve : With each video, focus on enhancing the hook and transition for smooth, engaging content.\n\nChecklist for Successful Video Shorts\nClear Audio : Ensure your voice is easy to understand by minimizing background noise.\nThis keeps the focus on your message and helps viewers stay engaged.\nGood Lighting : Record in a well-lit area so you’re clearly visible. Proper lighting adds professionalism and keeps your audience focused on you.\nAvoid the \"Pause\"\n: Start speaking immediately. Edit out any initial silence at the beginning of your video to make sure your voice starts the moment the video plays, capturing attention instantly.\nImmediate Movement : Begin with a visual movement to draw the viewer’s eye, like walking, jumping, or a slight camera shake. This adds energy and keeps viewers interested.\nStrong Hook : Capture attention within the first 2-3 seconds with a compelling statement or question that resonates with your audience. This helps prevent viewers from scrolling away.\nKeep It Short : Aim for a video length of 45-90 seconds to maintain viewer interest.\nShort, punchy content is more likely to be watched to the end.\nNatural Environment : Film while doing everyday activities, such as walking, getting coffee, or engaging with your surroundings. This makes your content feel relatable and unscripted.\nRelevant Hashtags : Use 6 specific hashtags relevant to your audience and message to increase discoverability and boost engagement.\nConsistent Posting : Post at least three times per week, with daily posts as the ideal goal. Regular posting builds momentum and grows your audience.\nCustom CTA : Make your call to action relevant to your message or activity in the video.\nThis helps guide viewers toward taking the next step.\nEngaging Thumbnails : Choose or create a captivating thumbnail to draw viewers in. A great thumbnail can make your video stand out among the rest.\nSubtitles (Optional) : Add subtitles for accessibility and to ensure viewers can follow along even if they’re watching without sound.\nReview Before Posting : Watch your video to catch any mistakes or awkward pauses.\nReviewing helps ensure that your video flows smoothly and captures attention from start to finish.\nMastering video hooks gives your content the best chance to reach and impact your audience effectively. Practice this framework, refine it, and watch your engagement grow."}]$wb_book$::jsonb
  ),
